# tools/al/ales_stereo_pad_tail_witness.rish -- stereo_pad_tail: the padded top-and-tail carried into stereo -- trim the leading and trailing joint silence like ALES150, yet keep a chosen margin of joint silence at each end, on both channels in lockstep (ALES151).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES151.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-054529_fill-ales151-lotus-stereo-pad-tail.md
# Stands on: lotus/stereo_top_tail.rye (ALES150 -- the flush stereo top-and-tail, the pad-zero twin, imported by the selftest and its StereoTopTailError reused whole), lotus/stereo_segment.rye (ALES147 -- the stereo segmenter, run
# once over one shared frame grid), lotus/segment.rye (ALES95 -- the mono segmenter and max_segments, through ALES147), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 -- the StereoClip and its balance invariant), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 -- the Clip and
# its cut), lotus/voiced.rye (ALES94 -- the .silent verdict this intersects). A Lotus master is a StereoClip whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length; ALES147 proved the two channels segment
# independently, so padding each channel by its own silence would give the two speakers different margins and lengths and tear the stereo image apart.
#
# lotus/stereo_pad_tail.rye keeps ONE margin for both channels -- the leading and trailing JOINT silence (a run of frames silent in both channels): segment both channels through ALES147, find the first and last frame not
# jointly-silent (the content bracket, the union of the two speakers' outer content, exactly ALES150's), keep min(pad, available) of the leading and trailing joint silence and cut only the excess from both channels (trailing
# first, so the leading index stays valid). The flush stereo trim is exactly pad = 0; every interior pause and the kept margin stay untouched on both channels. It validates the shared frame length, band, span, and floor once
# through ALES147's own up-front checks before any cut, so a refusal never trims one channel and leaves the other; StereoTopTailError (StereoSegmentError and EditError) reused whole. The crux is two laws -- the pad-zero law
# (stereo_top_and_tail_pad(pad=0) equals ALES150's flush stereo_top_and_tail byte for byte on both channels) and the margin law (the kept margin at each end is exactly min(pad, available), the result equal to the original over
# the padded span per channel, both channels balanced, monotone in pad, a pad past both ends leaving the master unchanged).
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_stereo_pad_tail_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-pad-tail: Language EN — stereo_pad_tail trims the leading and trailing joint silence from both channels of a stereo master like ALES150, yet keeps up to pad samples of joint silence adjacent to the content at each end, on both channels in lockstep. It segments both channels through ALES147's stereo segmenter over one shared frame grid, finds the first and last frame that is not jointly-silent (the content bracket, the union of the two speakers' outer content), keeps min(pad, available) of the leading and trailing joint silence, and cuts only the excess from both channels through ALES2's cut, trailing excess first so the leading index stays valid. A Lotus master is two Clips heard together, one per speaker, whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length; the kept margin is the shared joint silence, so both channels keep the same margin and leave the same length. It validates the shared frame length, band, span, and floor once before any cut so a refusal never trims one channel and leaves the other."
say "ales-stereo-pad-tail: Style Radiant — ALES150 cuts the joint dead air flush against the first and last frame either speaker fills. That is right for a sample library and wrong for a spoken take — a cut that lands exactly on the first consonant clips the attack and reads abrupt, so every recorder and DAW lets a keeper keep a margin. ALES99 added that one honest parameter to the mono trimmer, pad; this rung carries it into stereo. The wall is the one ALES148, ALES149, and ALES150 all met: ALES147 proved the two channels segment independently, so padding each channel by its own silence would give the two speakers different margins and lengths and tear the stereo image apart. This rung keeps one margin for both — the leading and trailing joint silence, kept up to pad and cut for the excess, identically from both channels. The crux is two laws. The pad-zero law: stereo_top_and_tail_pad with pad zero equals ALES150's flush stereo_top_and_tail byte for byte on both channels, the flush trim exactly the zero-margin case. The margin law: the result equals the original over the content bracket grown by min(pad, available) of the joint silence at each end, byte for byte on both channels — the kept margin exactly min(pad, available), a pad larger than the available joint silence keeping all of it and no more (never inventing a sample), a pad past both ends leaving the master unchanged, monotone in pad, both channels balanced. A known pad keeps exactly the named samples; an all-jointly-silent master trims to empty whatever the pad, a no-joint-silent master is unchanged, an empty clip and a ragged frame length and an inverted band and an illegal floor each refuse by name with both channels untouched."
say "ales-stereo-pad-tail: Lens TAME — two bounded i16 Clips (left, right) trimmed at the two ends in lockstep by one shared frame length, band, floor, split, and pad; the balance invariant is asserted on entry and exit, and all validation runs once up front through ALES147's segment_stereo (ALES92's precheck for band and span, the floor against meter's sample_peak_max, the frame-length divisibility) read-only before a single sample is cut, so a refusal leaves both channels untouched; the forward pass advances one monotonic cursor per channel across its own tiling run list, marks a frame jointly-silent only where it is silent in both, and records the first and last not-jointly-silent frame, asserting each cursor names the run that covers the frame start; the leading and trailing joint silence are computed from that content bracket, min(pad, available) kept at each end, the trailing excess cut first through ALES2's cut then the leading, the in-bounds span asserted before each cut so neither can fault and leave the pair half-trimmed, and the postcondition asserts both channels hold the content plus the two kept margins and leave equal length; the pad-zero law is proven against ALES150's flush stereo top-and-tail across eight frame lengths, four floors, and three splits, the margin law against an independent by-hand reference (a second implementation reading the frame verdicts by run scan) across eight pads, a known pad keeps the named samples, a pad past both ends leaves the master unchanged, pad is monotone, and the degenerate and atomicity cases leave both channels untouched and balanced; StereoTopTailError (StereoSegmentError and EditError) reused whole, siloed to lotus/, edits only by removing whole frames, fabricating no samples and reordering nothing, no adaptive floor, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_pad_tail"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_pad_tail.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-pad-tail: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_pad_tail.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-pad-tail: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-pad-tail: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-pad-tail' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-pad-tail: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-pad-tail line"
